<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Learning to Manage Time – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Study Habits for Success</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Learning to Manage Time</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe familiar processes or events. Interactive: Paraphrase a simple statement on familiar topics during discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe a personal study routine, outlining steps in logical order and giving specific examples (e.g., "First, I organize my notes by subject. Then, I review my math exercises. Finally, I test myself with flashcards.").
            Can paraphrase feedback on study habits during a discussion (e.g., "So you're saying that I have changed my study habits?").
            Can suggest study habit improvements based on past experiences and provide reasons for their effectiveness (e.g., "I used to forget assignments, so I started using a planner. Now, I submit my work on time and feel less stressed.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by asking students to stand up and physically simulate a quick routine related to studying (e.g., "Organize your backpack," "Open a book," "Write a note"). Then, students sit down and, in pairs, briefly discuss one positive study habit they <i>have developed</i> since the beginning of the school year. The teacher encourages using vocabulary like "organize," "manage," "focus," and "achievements." For example, "I have developed the habit of organizing my notes every day."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences using present perfect for achievements and past simple for reflection. For example, "I <i>used to procrastinate</i> on my assignments, but now I <i>have learned to prioritize</i> my tasks and manage my time better. This <i>has improved</i> my productivity." The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of "-ed" endings for past participles (e.g., /d/ in "improved," /t/ in "accomplished") and stresses the main verb.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'I used to forget my books,' am I talking about now or the past?" (Expected answer: "The past.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'I have achieved my study goals,' does it mean I'm still working on them or I've completed them?" (Expected answer: "You've completed them, it's an achievement.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Study Habits for Success"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio dialogue (2-3 minutes) between two high school students discussing their changed study habits and time management strategies. The dialogue features expressions of achievements ("I have successfully organized my weekly schedule"), past habits ("I used to waste a lot of time"), WH-questions ("What have you done to improve your focus?"), and vocabulary such as "productivity," "routines," "prioritize," and "effective."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student comprehends and interprets oral messages related to personal experiences and academic life, specifically identifying descriptions of processes or events and paraphrasing information from a discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are the two students mainly discussing?" (Students listen and report orally in pairs).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Oral):</b> The teacher reads 3-4 statements related to the dialogue (e.g., "Student A has started using a planner daily. True/False?"). Students verbally confirm True or False and correct false statements, explaining why.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Pair Discussion):</b> Students are given a list of study problems (e.g., "forgetting assignments," "low grades") and a list of solutions (e.g., "using a planner," "setting goals"). They discuss which solution matches which problem based on the dialogue.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice expressing achievements using the present perfect, reflecting on past habits with past simple, and asking/answering WH-questions related to study habits and time management. They will integrate vocabulary like "develop," "improve," "organize," "procrastinate," "assignments," "productivity," and "strategies." Pronunciation practice will focus on contrastive sounds (/sk/, /st/, /s/, /t/) and "-ed" endings, emphasizing stress on main verbs (e.g., "have DEVELOped").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Sentence Completion (Controlled Oral Practice):</b> The teacher provides sentence stems on the board or projector, focusing on grammar and vocabulary. Students complete them orally in pairs. Example stems: "I used to ___________ (verb), but now I have ___________ (verb in past participle) my ___________ (noun)." or "What have you done to __________ (verb) your ___________ (noun)?" After completing, pairs share one or two examples with the class, focusing on clear pronunciation of target sounds and verb stress.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. "Chain Dialogue" (Semi-controlled Substitution Drill):</b> Students form a circle. The first student states a past habit: "I used to procrastinate on my assignments." The next student paraphrases and then adds an achievement: "So you used to procrastinate. Well, I have developed a better study routine." The third student paraphrases the second student and adds their own: "So you've developed a routine. I used to forget my notes, but now I have learned to organize them." This continues around the circle, encouraging spontaneous use of the target structures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "Habit Interview" (Pair Interaction):</b> In pairs, students interview each other using prepared WH-questions on flashcards or a worksheet. Questions include: "What study habit have you changed recently?" "How has that improved your academic results?" "What did you use to do that you don't do anymore?" "What strategies could you suggest to a classmate to manage time more effectively?" Students take turns asking and answering, actively listening and paraphrasing their partner's answers to confirm understanding (e.g., "So, you're saying that you have set new goals?").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Study Habits for Success"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Study Habits Consultant Role-Play"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in groups of three. One student acts as a "student seeking advice" who has poor time management skills and study habits (e.g., "I used to procrastinate a lot; my grades have suffered"). The other two students act as "Study Habits Consultants." The consultants must ask WH-questions (e.g., "What have you done to manage your time?"), reflect on the student's past habits (e.g., "So, you used to waste time on social media?"), and suggest improvements based on effective time management techniques (e.g., "You should try using the Pomodoro technique. I have found it really helps maintain focus."). They must also describe a personal study routine, outlining steps in logical order. The "student seeking advice" should paraphrase the consultants' suggestions to show understanding (e.g., "So you're suggesting I schedule breaks?"). Each group is encouraged to use a variety of target vocabulary (e.g., "goals," "strategies," "productivity," "improvement") and grammatical structures. The teacher circulates, offering support and noting fluency, accuracy, and collaborative interaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 13 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Peer Assessment and Teacher Observation Checklist</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> During the "Study Habits Consultant Role-Play," students complete a short peer assessment checklist for their group members, evaluating their participation, use of target language, and ability to give/receive advice. The teacher uses an observation checklist to note individual student performance, focusing on specific criteria.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of key vocabulary and "-ed" endings (e.g., "improved," "developed").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of present perfect for achievements (e.g., "I have organized my tasks").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of past simple to reflect on past habits (e.g., "I used to procrastinate").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to ask and answer WH-questions relevant to study habits.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to paraphrase statements during discussion (e.g., "So, you're saying...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of ideas when describing routines and suggesting improvements.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active collaboration and engagement in group discussion.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts individually before leaving:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new strategy for time management I discussed today was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I still need to practice using ___________ (grammar point/vocabulary) when I speak about my study habits."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of today's speaking activities helped you the most in expressing your ideas about study habits?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can the study habits and time management strategies we discussed today positively impact your grades and overall productivity?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The "Chain Dialogue" and "Study Habits Consultant Role-Play" activities worked very well in promoting active speaking and interaction, allowing students to practice grammar and vocabulary in a communicative context. The focus on paraphrasing helped students actively listen. Some students still struggled with consistent use of the present perfect; perhaps more targeted drills or sentence starters could be integrated earlier. I need to provide more explicit feedback on specific pronunciation points during the practice stage next time. For next lesson, I will scaffold the writing task by reviewing sentence structures for describing processes and linking ideas coherently, building on the speaking practice from today.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The speaking practice in this lesson will directly inform Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), where students will write a descriptive paragraph about their personal study routine and suggested improvements, using the vocabulary and grammar practiced today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>